## The foundation we build on

### original-el8 — DGX Station GB300 research

The M3 lane comes from **original-el8/dgx-station-gb300-research**, snapshot
commit `87af662dd0a70f72c250c2085cf12eea2be8d882` (2026-10-07). It is
**Apache-2.0**. Its `upstream/` tree gave us the shape of the launcher, the
MegaMoE peer hook, the peer-tier sidecar, the rowmap builders, the calibration
sets, the fi-tune files and the vLLM #58132 overlay. The Apache-2.0 licence
covers the hook, the rowmaps and the fi-tune files; the `LICENSE` and `SOURCE`
are included under `fastpath/hook/`.

The **original-el8 lane** wrote the MegaMoE peer hook and much of the peer-tier work. The
hook is the part that makes one GB300 and one RTX PRO 6000 behave like one
machine: hot experts stay on the GB300, cold experts live on the RTX, and the
two sides meet through a small pinned-memory protocol. Its rowmap tooling and
calibration sets are the reason the split is balanced rather than guessed. We
are grateful for both the code and the clear write-ups.

If you redistribute the `upstream/` tree, keep its Apache-2.0 `LICENSE`, its
`SOURCE` file and this notice. The files also carry their own headers where
they have one.

### vLLM

**vLLM** is **Apache-2.0** and is the serving engine underneath everything.
We pin the nightly image
`vllm/vllm-openai:nightly-af7f9488c2210d67e1033ecdc845b087ee7fe92b`.

The `overlay-58132/` patch is vLLM pull request
[#58132](https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm/pull/58132), restricted to its
runtime files. The `overlay-1017/` frontend is derived from the image's vLLM
files and the checkpoint's own encoder; the files that carry
`SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0` remain Apache-2.0. The fastpath hooks
that patch vLLM classes are our original code unless a header says otherwise.

Thank you to the vLLM team, especially the DeepSeek-V4.1 model, speculative
decoding and frontend maintainers, and to the authors and reviewers of
#58132. Their engine made this recipe possible.

### local-inference-lab

**local-inference-lab**'s vLLM fork and their notes on serving large MoE
models on a single box shaped many of the decisions here: what "good" looks
like at one stream, which parts of the stack are worth touching, and where the
fixed costs really are. We did not vendor their vLLM fork; their b12x
snapshot is vendored separately under `docker/b12x/` with its licence. Their
vLLM fork is Apache-2.0. Thank you for publishing the notes as well as the
patches.

### FlashInfer and b12x

**FlashInfer** is **Apache-2.0**. It provides the low-latency MXFP8 GEMM and
the SM120 fused-MoE path used by the RTX sidecar, and several of our kernels
follow its CuTe DSL and TRT-LLM patterns. Thank you to the FlashInfer team and
the kernel authors who made those paths public.

**b12x** started as **Local Inference Lab**'s standalone SM120 inference
stack and has moved into FlashInfer's experimental tree:
<https://github.com/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/tree/main/flashinfer/experimental/b12x>.
The standalone upstream is
<https://github.com/local-inference-lab/b12x> at commit
`2cc7f66aaef76a9c7cbb660a1efb865cf3f26c88`; the same code now lives in
FlashInfer. b12x is **Apache-2.0**, with one BSD-3-Clause file
(`b12x/_lib/dense_gemm.py`, NVIDIA copyright) and other files carrying their
own notices. A **subset** of that upstream tree is vendored under
`docker/b12x/` for the image build: every copied file matches the pinned
commit (a byte-for-byte diff is clean), 159 upstream-only paths are not
shipped, and there are no local modifications. Its `LICENSE` and the
BSD-3-Clause notice must travel with it. If you rebuild the image, pin the
full commit and keep the snapshot's licence files.

### DeepGEMM

**DeepGEMM** is **MIT**, copyright 2025 DeepSeek. The MegaMoE kernel is
DeepGEMM's; our `megamoe_fork/` is a scheduling-only fork of two headers.
Every tile's math and the combine order stay stock. The fork keeps the
DeepGEMM MIT terms. The two modified headers now carry
`SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT` and `Copyright (c) 2025 DeepSeek`. DeepGEMM also carries CUTLASS headers under the
**BSD-3-Clause** licence; keep those notices if you redistribute the fork or
its generated sources.

### TileLang, PyTorch, CUDA and the rest of the stack

- **TileLang** is **MIT**; many of our kernels are written in it.
- **PyTorch** is **BSD-3-Clause**; the image and the extensions link it.
- **NVIDIA CUDA / cuBLAS / CUTLASS / TensorRT-LLM** components are covered by
  NVIDIA's licences and the BSD-3-Clause CUTLASS notice. They are dependencies,
  not ours to relicense.
- **DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash-1017** weights are released by DeepSeek under
  **MIT**. The checkpoint's own `LICENSE` is the MIT text, copyright 2023
  DeepSeek; we verified it in the downloaded checkpoint. The official model
  card is:
  <https://huggingface.co/deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash-1017>. We do not
  re-upload weights. The checkpoint's `encoding/encoding.py` is included in
  the 1017 frontend overlay; the checkpoint `LICENSE` (MIT, Copyright 2023
  DeepSeek) covers it. Keep the MIT notice with it.
- **GSM8K** is OpenAI's grade-school-math dataset, released under **MIT**.
  The RonanLabs harness copy used by the benchmark is **Apache-2.0** and keeps
  its `LICENSE.ronanlabs` and `SOURCE.ronanlabs`; keep both. Thank you to the
  GSM8K authors and to RonanLabs for the test file.

### RonanLabs — the sibling launcher

**RonanLabsAI/dgx-station-frontier** is the closest sibling to this recipe.
We learned the stale-buffer cleanup, the FlashInfer JIT cache mount and the
bring-up gate from their launcher, and their public results gave us a sanity
check on the same hardware lane. Their repository is **Apache-2.0** for the
whole tree, which covers those launcher ideas as well as the GSM8K harness.
The harness's `LICENSE.ronanlabs` and `SOURCE` are included under
`verify/data/`; keep both. Thank you.

### BRRR — the fixed bench prompt

The single-stream prompt of `bench --full` (`verify/data/fixed_prompt.txt`) is
the coding task from **Fraser Price's BRRR** bench
(<https://huggingface.co/fraserprice/DeepSeek-4.1-Flash-1017-BRRR>,
`src/brrr/bench.py`), repeated ten times. BRRR is **Apache-2.0**; its
`LICENSE.brrr` and `SOURCE.brrr` are included under `verify/data/`; keep both.
Using the same prompt means single-stream numbers can be read against that
bench's. Thank you.

